\begin_layout Title
Game Theory: Homework 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Prove that if a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 matrix has a 
\emph on
strict
\emph default
 Nash equilibrium that is also unique, then the game can be solved via iterated
 strict dominance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume that a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 matrix has a 
\emph on
strict 
\emph default
Nash equilibrium that is also unique.
 Then, this Nash equilibrium must be a pure strategy profile.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{*}=(s_{1}^{*},s_{2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 be the unique pure strategy Nash equilibrium.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N,\ \forall\ s_{i}\neq s_{i}^{*}:u_{i}(s_{i}^{*},s_{j}^{*})>u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j}^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Since each player has two pure strategies, then 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{1}^{0}=\{s_{1}^{*},s_{1}\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}^{0}=\{s_{2}^{*},s_{2}\}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(s_{1},s_{2})$
\end_inset

 is not a Nash equilibrium, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ i\in N:u_{i}(s_{i}^{*},s_{j})>u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(s_{1}^{*},s_{2})>u_{1}(s_{1},s_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 We now have that 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(s_{1}^{*},s_{2}^{*})>u_{1}(s_{1},s_{2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(s_{1}^{*},s_{2})>u_{1}(s_{1},s_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

, so it can be eliminated by iterated strict dominance.
 In the next iteration, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{1}^{1}=\left\{ s_{1}^{*}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}^{1}=\left\{ s_{2}^{*},s_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(s_{2}^{*},s_{1}^{*})>u_{2}(s_{2},s_{1}^{*})$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2}$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

, so it can be eliminated by iterated strict dominance.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $(s_{1}^{*},s_{2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 is the only strategy profile that survives iterated elimination of dominated
 strategies.
\end_layout

