\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Verify that no pure-strategy NE of this game exists
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compute the mixed-strategy NE, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\sigma_{1}^{*}(H),\sigma_{2}^{*}(H)\right)$
\end_inset

 of this game.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain briefly (in economic terms) why the NE does not depend on the parameters
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For player 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(H,H)>u_{1}(T,H)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(T,T)>u_{1}(H,T)$
\end_inset

.
 For player 2, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(T,H)>u_{2}(H,H)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(H,T)>u_{2}(T,T)$
\end_inset

.
 So, in this game, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ s\in S,\ \exists\ i\in N,\ \exists\ s_{i}^{\prime}\in S_{i}:u(s_{i},s_{-i})<u(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nexists\ s\in S,\ \forall\ i\in N,\ \forall\ s_{i}^{\prime}:u_{i}(s_{i},s_{-i})\geq u_{i}(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, no pure-strategy Nash Equilibrium of this game exists.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even though there is no pure-strategy Nash Equilibrium of this game, we
 know that every finite strategic-form game has a mixed strategy Nash Equilibriu
m.
 So, we wish to find the mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 be a mixed strategy employed by player 1 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1H}=\sigma_{1}(H)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\sigma_{1H})=\sigma_{1}(T)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 be a mixed strategy employed by player 2 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2H}=\sigma_{2}(H)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\sigma_{2H})=\sigma_{2}(T)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Eu_{1}(H|\sigma_{2H})=a_{1}\sigma_{2H}-a_{1}(1-\sigma_{2H})=2a_{1}\sigma_{2H}-a_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Eu_{1}(T|\sigma_{2H})=-a_{1}\sigma_{2H}+a_{1}(1-\sigma_{2H})=-2a_{1}\sigma_{2H}+a_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Eu_{1}(H|\sigma_{2H}) & = & Eu_{1}(T|\sigma_{2H})\\
2a_{1}\sigma_{2H}-a_{1} & = & -2a_{1}\sigma_{2H}+a_{1}\\
4a_{1}\sigma_{2H} & = & 2a_{1}\\
\sigma_{2H} & = & \frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Analogously, the same result can be obtained for player 2.
 Thus, the mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{1}^{*},\sigma_{2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1H}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2H}^{*}=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since utility is ordinal, the Nash Equilibrium does not depend on the exact
 values of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{1}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Two students 
\begin_inset Formula $(i=1,2)$
\end_inset

, each having an endowment of time 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, are working on a project together.
 Each student 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has to decide how to allocate his time endowment between working 
\begin_inset Formula $(t_{i})$
\end_inset

 and leisure 
\begin_inset Formula $(l_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The grade each receives on the project depends on how much time they spend
 collectively working on it.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $G=50(t_{1}+t_{2})$
\end_inset

 denote the grade earned on the project.
 The utility function of student 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(G,l_{i})=a_{i}\sqrt{2G}+l_{i}
\]

\end_inset

The students simultaneously choose how much time they will allocate to the
 project.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Set up the optimization problem for player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and derive the best response function 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}^{br}(t_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{10}>a_{1}>a_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Carefully draw the best response functions with 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}$
\end_inset

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

-axis.
 Characterize all Nash equilibria (if any) in pure strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}=a_{2}=a<\frac{1}{10}$
\end_inset

.
 Characterize all Nash equilibria (if any) in pure strategies.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the best response function for player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we maximize his utility function taking the strategy of player 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 as given.
 So, the optimization problem for player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{t_{i}}\ u_{i}(G,l_{i})=a_{i}\sqrt{100\left(t_{i}+t_{j}\right)}+(1-t_{i})
\]

\end_inset

We obtain the first-order condition and solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{\partial u_{i}(G,l_{i})}{\partial l_{i}}=0\iff\frac{1}{2}a_{i}\left[100\left(t_{i}+t_{2}\right)\right]^{-\frac{1}{2}}(100)-1=0\\
5a_{i}\left(t_{i}+t_{j}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}=1\\
t_{i}=25a_{i}^{2}-t_{j}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, the best response function of person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
t_{i}^{br}(t_{j})=\begin{cases}
25a_{i}^{2}-t_{j} & \mbox{if}\ t_{j}\leq25a_{i}^{2}\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ t_{j}>25a_{i}^{2}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Graph.png

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pure strategy Nash Equilibrium is 
\begin_inset Formula $(t_{1},t_{2})=(25a_{1}^{2},0)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename Graph1.png

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under the imposed condition, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}^{*}=t_{1}^{br}(t_{2}^{*})=25a^{2}-t_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{2}^{*}=t_{2}^{br}(t_{1}^{*})=25a^{2}-t_{1}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{1}^{*}+t_{2}^{*}=25a^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a first-price sealed-bid auction of an object with two bidders.
 Each bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's valuation of the object is 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is known to both bidders.
 The auction rules are that each player submits a bid in a sealed envelope.
 The envelopes are then opened, and the bidder who has submitted the highest
 bid gets the object and pays the auctioneer the amount of his bid.
 If the bidders submit the same bid, each gets the object with probablitity
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Bids must be in dollar muliples (assume that valuations are also).
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Are any strategies strictly dominated?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Are any strategies weakly dominated?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Is there a Nash Equilibrium? What is it? Is it unique?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the bid person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 submits.
 The utility function for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})=\begin{cases}
v_{i}-b_{i} & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{i}>b_{j}}}\\
\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}-b_{i}) & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{i}=b_{j}}}\\
0 & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{i}<b_{j}}}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}\in S_{i}\equiv\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not strictly dominated.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly dominated.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ b_{i}^{\prime}\neq b_{i},\ \forall\ b_{j}\in S_{j}:u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})>u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i},b_{i}+1)=0=u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}+1)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}=b_{i}+1$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}^{\prime}>b_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i},b_{i}^{\prime}+1)=0=u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{i}^{\prime}+1)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}=b_{i}^{\prime}+1$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, neither of the players have any strictly dominated strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, a strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 is weakly dominated if 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\; b_{i}^{\prime}\neq b_{i}\in\mathbb{N},\;\forall\; b_{j}\in\mathbb{N}:u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})\geq u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}\geq v_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}$
\end_inset

 is weakly dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Case

\series bold
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}>b_{j}$
\end_inset


\series default
.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})=v_{i}-b_{i}^{\prime}>0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})=v_{i}-b_{i}\leq0$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}=b_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})=\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}-b_{i}^{\prime})>0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})=v_{i}-b_{i}\leq0$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}>b_{j}>b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})=0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $u(b_{i},b_{j})=v_{i}-b_{i}\leq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}\geq b_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i}^{\prime},b_{j})=0$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})=v_{i}-b_{i}\leq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}\geq v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is weakly dominated by
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>b_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, under certain conditions, zero is also weakly dominated
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(0,b_{j})=\begin{cases}
\frac{v_{i}}{2} & \mbox{if}\ b_{j}=0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ b_{j}>0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(b_{i},b_{j})=\begin{cases}
v_{i}-b_{i} & \mbox{if}\ b_{j}=0\\
v_{i}-b_{i} & \mbox{if}\ 0<b_{j}<b_{i}\\
\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}-b_{i}) & \mbox{if}\ b_{j}=b_{i}\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ b_{j}>b_{i}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{v_{i}}{2}\geq b_{i}$
\end_inset

, then zero is weakly dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}\in(0,v_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Nash Equilibrium is a set of mutual best responses.
 The best response function for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b_{i}^{*}=r_{i}(b_{j})=\begin{cases}
[0,b_{j}) & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{j}>v_{i}}}\\
{}[0,b_{j}] & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{j}=v_{i}}}\\
v_{i}-1 & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{j}=v_{i}-1}}\\
\left\{ v_{1}-1,v_{1}-2\right\}  & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{j}=v_{i}-2}}\\
b_{j}+1 & \mbox{if \ensuremath{b_{j}<v_{i}-2}}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Without loss of generality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1}\geq v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We will now consider the different cases for 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}=v_{1}=v$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v,v)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v-1,v-1)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(v-2,v-2)$
\end_inset

 are all Nash Equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}=v_{1}-1$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2},v_{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2},v_{2}-1)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2}-1,v_{2}-1)$
\end_inset

 are all Nash Equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}=v_{1}-2$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2}+1,v_{2})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2},v_{2})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2},v_{2}-1)$
\end_inset

 are all Nash Equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}<v_{1}-2$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2}+1,v_{2})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{2},v_{2}-1)$
\end_inset

 are both Nash Equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, we have found multiple Nash Equilibria in each case.
\end_layout

